course: world
lesson: 184
title: "If... then..." and its traps
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: "If it rains, the grass gets wet" is a one-way street: rain leads to wet grass. It doesn't run backwards, because wet grass can also come from a sprinkler, dew or a dog.
explain: Two traps catch people all the time: running the "if" backwards ("the grass is wet, so it rained") and "not the first, so not the second" ("my cat isn't a dog, so it's not an animal").
explain: One move is always safe: if every square has 4 sides, then a shape with 3 sides is not a square. Leo: "Bro, it has three sides. It's a triangle. It's not undercover."
explain: Even perfect steps give a wrong answer if the starting rule is false: "if the dog barks, someone's at the door" fails the day he barks at a squirrel.
explain: In everyday deals, "if you finish your work, you can play" usually means "only if you finish". Lucas knows the strict logic; the teacher still hands him a worksheet.
fact: "If it rains, the grass is wet" does not mean "if the grass is wet, it rained"; sprinklers, dew and dogs wet grass too.
fact: Running an "if" backwards is a mistake grown-ups call affirming the consequent.
fact: "If it's a dog, it's an animal" says nothing about things that aren't dogs: cats, horses and people are animals too.
fact: If all squares have 4 sides, then any shape without 4 sides is not a square; this move is always right.
fact: A shape with 4 sides is not always a square: rectangles and kites have 4 sides too.
fact: "If you're in Year 5, you swim on Tuesdays" tells you about Year 5, not about everyone who swims on Tuesday.
fact: "If it's sunny, we'll go to the park" says nothing about rainy days, so going anyway is not breaking the promise.
fact: In strict logic "if A, then B" says nothing about what happens when A is false; in everyday deals like "if you finish, you can play", people usually mean "only if".
fact: A correct step from a false rule can give a wrong answer: a dog that barks at squirrels doesn't prove someone is at the door.
fact: Studying makes passing more likely, but passing doesn't prove you studied: an easy test is another reason.
fact: "If you are under 12, you must wear armbands" only talks about under-12s; older swimmers may still choose to wear them.
fact: Words people use: "bruv" and "bro" (London, young people) mean mate; "mardy" (Midlands, North) means sulky; "wee" (Scotland) means small; "aye" (Scotland, North) means yes; "Roblox" is a computer game; "my bad" means "my mistake".
exercise: Ruby sees wet grass: "If it rains, the grass gets wet. The grass is wet, so it rained." Her dad has just turned off the sprinkler. What is her mistake? | Running the "if" backwards | Using a false rule about rain | Hearing "some" as "all" in the rule | Attacking the person, not the idea
exercise: Which of these can also make grass wet? | A sprinkler, dew or a dog | Only rain falling from the sky | Only a hosepipe, nothing else | Nothing except a big storm
exercise: Kai's friend: "If something is a dog, it's an animal. My cat isn't a dog, so it's not an animal." What is wrong? | Many things that aren't dogs are still animals | Cats are really a kind of dog | The rule about dogs is false | Nothing; the reasoning is right
exercise: "A square has 4 sides. Leo's shape has 3 sides. So it's not a square." Is Leo right? | Yes; a shape without 4 sides can't be a square | No; you can never tell what a shape is from its sides | No; some triangles are squares | Yes, but only for small shapes
exercise: Leo's friend has a shape with 4 sides. Must it be a square? | No; rectangles and kites have 4 sides too | Yes; every 4-sided shape is a square | Yes; that follows straight from the square rule | No; squares have 5 sides
exercise: The pool rule: "If you're in Year 5, you swim on Tuesdays." Ellie sees Sam swimming on Tuesday. What can she be sure of? | Nothing about Sam's year | Sam must be in Year 5 | Sam can't be in Year 5 | Sam is breaking the rule
exercise: Mum: "If it's sunny, we'll go to the park." It rains, and they still go in raincoats. Did Mum break her promise? | No; she said nothing about rainy days | Yes; she said they'd go only if sunny | Yes; "if" always means "only if" | No, because it was sunny somewhere else
exercise: Teacher: "If you finish your work, you can play a game." Lucas doesn't finish: "You never said 'only if'!" What is true? | In strict logic he has a point, but deals like this usually mean "only if" | He is wrong in logic and in everyday talk | A teacher's "if" never means "only if" | "If" and "only if" always mean the same
exercise: Leo: "If the dog barks, someone's at the door. He's barking, so someone's there." Nobody is; a squirrel runs off. What went wrong? | The rule "barking means someone's at the door" was false | The step from the rule was wrong | Squirrels can't make dogs bark | Leo opened the wrong door
exercise: Amira: "If I study, I'll pass." She passes. "See, I studied!" What is wrong? | Passing can have other reasons, like an easy test | Passing always proves that you studied hard enough | Studying makes people fail | Easy tests can't be passed
exercise: A sign: "If you are under 12, you must wear armbands in the pool." Finn is 13 and wears them. Is he breaking the rule? | No; the rule only talks about under-12s | Yes; over-12s must not wear armbands at all | Yes; you must be under 12 to wear them at all | No, because rules don't count at swimming pools
exercise: Which move is always right? | If A always gives B, then no B means no A | If A gives B, then B means A | If A gives B, then no A always means no B either | If A gives B, then B means no A
exercise: What do grown-ups call running the "if" backwards? | Affirming the consequent | A straw man | Moving the goalposts mid-game | A false dilemma
exercise: Which is a true "if" that does NOT run backwards? | If it's a cat, it's an animal; but an animal isn't always a cat | If it's an animal, it's a cat | If it's not a cat, it's not an animal | If it's a cat, it's not an animal
test: Ruby's dad: "I've just been watering it, Ruby. The rain didn't send a postcard." What is he teaching her? | Wet grass doesn't prove rain; other things wet grass | Wet grass always proves that it rained, whatever Dad says | Sprinklers can't make grass wet | Postcards tell you the weather
test: Kai: "Your cat's not an animal? What is it then, a sofa with a tail?" Which reasoning is Kai laughing at? | "Not a dog, so not an animal" | "All dogs are animals" | "Cats are animals" | "Sofas are furniture, not animals"
test: Leo: "Bruv, it's got three sides. It's a triangle." His friend: "You can't know it's not a square!" Who is right? | Leo; without 4 sides it can't be a square | The friend; it might be a square in disguise | The friend; you must measure the corners first | Nobody; shapes can change
test: Sam: "I'm in the swimming club, Ellie. I swim Tuesdays because I want to. I'm in Year 3." What mistake did Ellie make? | She ran the "if" backwards | She used the square rule | She judged Sam's swimming costume | She counted the wrong day
test: Josh, mardy: "You lied! You said sunny!" They went to the park in the rain. Who is right? | Mum; she only promised what happens on sunny days | Josh; "if sunny" means they never go on rainy days | Josh; Mum broke her word | Nobody; parks close in the rain
test: Teacher: "A logic professor would give you a point, Lucas. I'm giving you a worksheet." Why both? | Strictly he has a point, but everyone knew the deal meant "only if" | He is wrong in logic, so he gets a worksheet | Logic professors don't believe in games | The teacher doesn't understand logic
test: Mum: "Your logic's perfect, Leo. Your dog's isn't. He barks at leaves." What made Leo's answer wrong? | A false starting rule | A bad step in the reasoning | Opening the door too slowly | Leaves being hard to see
test: Amira's brother: "You played Roblox all week and passed. The test passed you, not the other way round." What is he pointing out? | Passing doesn't prove studying; the test was easy | Playing games all week is what makes you pass tests | Amira must have studied in secret | No test can ever be easy
test: A Scottish lifeguard: "He's 13 and wearing the wee armbands. So what? Being extra safe's not breaking a rule, aye?" Why is the lifeguard right? | The rule only says what under-12s must do | Armbands are banned for everyone over 12 | The rule says over-12s must wear them | Only lifeguards may decide about armbands
test: What would happen if Leo's dog only ever barked when someone was at the door? | Then "he's barking, so someone's there" would work | Leo's reasoning would still be wrong, whatever the dog did | Squirrels would stop coming to the garden near the door | The dog would stop barking at anything ever again
test: In a cartoon a detective sees wet footprints in the hall and shouts, "It rained indoors!" Put the cartoon right. | Wet footprints could come from a bath, a puddle or a spill | Wet footprints only come from indoor rain | Footprints can never tell you anything | It often rains inside houses
exam: It is given that the proposition "if it rains, the grass is wet" holds and that the grass is observed to be wet. Select the response that is consistent with the established facts. | It cannot be concluded that it has rained, since other causes may wet the grass | It may be concluded with certainty that it has rained | It may be concluded that it has not rained | The proposition is thereby shown to be false
exam: Identify the conclusion which validly follows from the premise that every square possesses four sides, given a figure possessing three sides. | The figure is not a square | The figure is a square | The figure may be a square | The figure possesses four sides
exam: A regulation provides that persons under twelve years of age shall wear armbands in the pool. Ascertain the position of a swimmer aged thirteen who elects to wear armbands. | The swimmer is in no breach of the regulation | The swimmer is in breach of the regulation | The swimmer is thereby deemed to be under twelve | The regulation prohibits armbands for such a swimmer
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an argument whose every step is correct but whose initial rule is false? | Its conclusion may nonetheless be false | Its conclusion is necessarily and always true | Its conclusion is necessarily false in every case | It cannot contain any correct step whatsoever
